1: # P3F.31. The OS49 positive-diagonal filling and its actual page
2: 
3: **Proved.** This statement concerns the positive-diagonal filling
4: defined below. It leaves the negative-diagonal family of P3F.26 intact.
5: The two fans have the common torus family and are related by the
6: specified toric flop; they are not identified as the same family.
7: 
8: This positive-diagonal filling gives an actual sphere family over
9: \(\lambda=r^3\), with zero positive-loop return index. The actual
10: page, circle-line comparison, boundary support, and cap marking
11: are constructed below. Root and fourfold independently audited
12: these maps and the relative cohomology argument before this
13: statement was marked proved.
14: 
15: ## 1. The exact line subfan
16: 
17: Keep the periods \(h=(2,1)\), \(g=(-3,-1)\) of P3F.26 and use
18: the periodic triangulation whose two triangles in a unit square are
19: \[
20:  (0,0),(1,0),(1,1),\qquad (0,0),(0,1),(1,1).          \tag{P3F.31.1}
21: \]
22: All cones at height one are unimodular, and the full period lattice
23: has index one. The compact fundamental domains of P3F.26 prove
24: smoothness, compactness and properness of this actual new filling.
25: The finite cap and the type-II line-complement quotient are unchanged.
26: Its integral topology can be compared by actual morphisms. Put
27: \(u_j\) for the height-one rays over the four corners of a unit
28: square. The common refinement adds the ray
29: \[
30:  v=u_1+u_2=u_0+u_4=(1,1,2).
31: \]
32: It is the blowup of the diagonal \(\mathbb P^1\) in either
33: small resolution. The normal bundle of each centre is
34: \(\mathcal O(-1)^{\oplus2}\), and the exceptional divisor is
35: \(\mathbb P^1\times\mathbb P^1\), with the two morphisms
36: contracting its two rulings. The equivariant period identifications
37: give these global morphisms on the two compact fillings; there is
38: one orbit of diagonal curves.
39: 
40: For this blowup, integral cohomology is the direct sum of pullback
41: cohomology and the Gysin image of \(H^{q-2}(\mathbb P^1;\mathbb Z)\).
42: One can verify the splitting by the long exact sequence of the
43: complement of the centre and the Thom isomorphism; integration
44: on the exceptional ruling, whose exceptional-divisor degree is
45: \(-1\), proves that the added generator is primitive. Thus the
46: only additional groups are one free generator in degrees two and
47: four. Van Kampen preserves the fundamental group: both local
48: pieces and their sphere-bundle boundary are simply connected.
49: Apply these facts to the two morphisms from the same refinement.
50: Since the negative-diagonal filling of P3F.26 is a simply connected
51: integral homology sphere, so is this positive-diagonal filling.
52: It is therefore the standard smooth six-sphere. This comparison
53: does not identify the two families' return maps.
54: 
55: Here is the additional property needed for the page. On the infinite
56: Tate chain of the source \(I_2\), put
57: \[
58:  h_j=\lceil j/2\rceil,
59:  \qquad h_{j+2}=h_j+1.
60: \]
61: The fan of the actual line complement \(M^\times\) has rays
62: \((j,h_j,1)\) and cones on consecutive pairs of these rays.
63: Projection to \((j,1)\) is the source Tate-chain projection. On
64: each such cone the total space is \(\mathbb C^2\times\mathbb C^*\);
65: the last factor is the delta circle's complexification. The line
66: degree on component \(j\) is
67: \[
68:  -(h_{j-1}+h_{j+1}-2h_j).
69: \]
70: The minus sign follows directly from fibre-coordinate transitions:
71: if the two adjacent slopes are \(s_-,s_+\), the fibre coordinates
72: on the two component charts differ by \(Z^{s_--s_+}\), which is
73: the inverse of the line-frame transition. The degrees are therefore
74: \((-1,+1)\), exactly those of \(M=\mathcal O(G-O)\).
75: The edges are alternately \((1,1)\) and \((1,0)\), so this is a
76: literal subfan of (P3F.31.1). Its quotient by \(h\) is the actual
77: \(M^\times\) over the whole source \(I_2\) neighbourhood,
78: including both nodes. This identifies its map into the filling by
79: toric charts, not merely by a boundary lattice.
80: 
81: It also checks the direction of the lift. Under \(g\), the graph
82: height defect is
83: \[
84:  h_j-1-h_{j-3}=0\quad(j\text{ even}),\qquad
85:  h_j-1-h_{j-3}=1\quad(j\text{ odd}).
86: \]
87: Thus the lift is invertible over source \(C_0\) and acquires its
88: simple zero over source \(C_1\), which is sent to target \(C_0\).
89: This is precisely the target-point linearization
90: \(\phi:T^*M\to M\) of P3F.28. Holomorphic units are retained
91: in the line frames and in both periods; they do not change any of
92: these chart inclusions.
93: 
94: For comparison, the negative-diagonal fan of P3F.26 has horizontal,
95: vertical, and negative-diagonal edges, and does not contain this
96: positive-diagonal line subfan. Flipping the diagonal of each unit
97: square gives the actual toric birational comparison, through the
98: common refinement. No diffeomorphism or equality of return indices
99: is concluded from that comparison here.
100: 
101: ## 2. A page carrying the actual source line
102: 
103: Choose a Hermitian norm on the source line which, on the regular
104: annulus bounding the cusp neighbourhood, is unitary flat on every
105: elliptic fibre. Such fibrewise metrics vary smoothly on that annulus;
106: interpolate their logarithms to any smooth metric on a smaller
107: neighbourhood of the central fibre. No flat extension across the
108: nonzero component degrees is imposed. The lift covering
109: \(-Q\), before the zero is removed, is a global holomorphic line
110: map on the compact source. Its operator norm is bounded. After
111: choosing \(|\lambda|\) sufficiently small, it decreases every
112: nonzero norm on its domain by a factor bounded above by a constant
113: \(c<1\). On a source cusp neighbourhood choose three radii
114: \(R_1<R_2<R_3\) with \(cR_3<R_1\). The three norm shells
115: \(\|v\|=R_j\) map to the filling through the actual line subfan.
116: No nonzero iterate of the contracting lift identifies two points
117: of those shells: every forward iterate that stays in the line
118: complement has norm at most \(cR_3\), and the inverse case is
119: the same assertion reversed. A zero cannot leave this open subset
120: and re-enter it through a later toric translate. Indeed the exact
121: height defect of \(g^N\), for \(N>0\), is
122: \[
123:  h_j-N-h_{j-3N}=
124:  \begin{cases}\lfloor N/2\rfloor&j\text{ even},\\
125:  \lceil N/2\rceil&j\text{ odd}.
126:  \end{cases}
127: \]
128: It is positive after the first zero; the same cone calculation
129: at a source node uses both endpoint defects. Thus no later
130: positive translate is a point of the line-complement subfan.
131: The period \(h\) has already been divided out in the
132: source line bundle. The resulting maps are therefore injective
133: immersions. On a compact closed cusp neighbourhood the shells
134: are compact, so these maps are embeddings.
135: 
136: Their delta quotients are three embedded copies of the actual
137: source \(I_2\) neighbourhood. Each misses the fixed sphere,
138: because the delta action on \(M^\times\) is free and the toric
139: open subset just specified misses the vertical fixed edge. On
140: each copy the actual delta bundle is exactly the chosen unit
141: circle bundle of \(M\), by the displayed map. This gives the
142: line identification through both nodes and both components.
143: 
144: Away from the cusp use the marked regular-binding page of the
145: affine circle quotient. The order-three finite cap has gamma
146: displacement \(1/3\); the corresponding circle base is the
147: weighted Hopf three-sphere of weights \((1,3)\). Its regular
148: binding page projects with degree three to the elliptic base,
149: branched at the order-three value and at the regular binding.
150: The type-II neighbourhood uses the original line-complement
151: quotient, so its source surface is retained. On the cubic root
152: of \(IV^*\), the source has the already verified smooth good
153: reduction. These are the same explicit root-chart and affine
154: translations as in P3F.26.
155: 
156: On this regular annulus the norm multiplier \(c(q)\) of the
157: lift is constant on each elliptic fibre. Indeed translation
158: pulls its unitary flat metric to another flat metric, and the
159: logarithm of the norm ratio of the holomorphic line isomorphism
160: is harmonic on the compact elliptic fibre, hence constant.
161: Consequently \(\log\|v\|/\log c(q)\pmod1\) is the actual
162: real suspension height, with the marked gamma cycle. The norm
163: shells can be interpolated to levels \(\|v\|=c(q)^{a_j}\),
164: where the three distinct \(a_j\) have the prescribed cyclic
165: order. Choose them in a sufficiently small interval that all
166: shell-separation inequalities remain strict on this compact
167: annulus. An orientation-preserving reparametrization of the
168: suspension circle then carries these levels to the three
169: regular-binding page levels. This reparametrization is over the
170: unchanged source and base coordinates, so it is a graph isotopy
171: of the actual suspension charts. There is an exact lift: put
172: \(t=\log\|v\|/\log c(q)\), lift the circle reparametrization to
173: \(F_q:\mathbb R\to\mathbb R\) with
174: \(F_q(t+1)=F_q(t)+1\), and send
175: \[
176:  v\longmapsto c(q)^{F_q(t)-t}v.
177: \]
178: Its multiplier is positive and one-periodic in \(t\). It commutes
179: with the contracting lift, keeps the source and base coordinates
180: fixed, and is delta-equivariant. Thus it carries the actual circle
181: line, including its gluing. Keep the inner norm shells and the outer page fixed
182: during the interpolation. This glues the three source cusp
183: neighbourhoods to the outside page with the stated boundary maps.
184: 
185: This constructs an embedded four-dimensional page \(V\subset Y\)
186: which misses the fixed sphere and has the original marked binding
187: \(T^3\). Its projection to the degree-three base page is still
188: the source elliptic fibration: the annular interpolation is over
189: the unchanged base coordinate. Its compact regular-fibre cap
190: has three \(II\) and three \(I_2\) fibres. The actual delta
191: line on \(V\) is the pullback section line \(M\), up to a base
192: line. To see the last assertion without discarding component
193: data, the comparison is the exact norm-shell map at each \(I_2\),
194: the original quotient map at each \(II\), and the good-reduction
195: root map at the finite cap. On the smooth pieces normalize the
196: fibrewise line isomorphism at the zero section; its overlap ratios
197: are base functions. The base page has boundary, so its line is
198: topologically trivial. The affine torsor origins can be chosen
199: over this base because its punctured-cell model is a graph and
200: the obstruction has no degree-two cochains.
201: 
202: ## 3. The full graph-to-page comparison by regular bands
203: 
204: Use the relative cusp product with primitive source cycles \((H,w)\)
205: and angular cycle \(K\). Its fixed-edge charts again have the
206: opposite normal weights and bundle \(\mathcal O(-1)^{\oplus2}\).
207: In detail, the two positive-fan charts along a vertical edge have
208: coordinates
209: \[
210:  (A,B,Z)=(q/V,V/Z,Z),\qquad
211:  (A',B',Z')=(qZ/V,V,1/Z),
212: \]
213: with \((A',B')=(ZA,ZB)\), and delta weights \((-1,+1,0)\).
214: For the opposite small resolution the orbit-product calculation
215: of P3F.9 is obtained by exchanging the two moving conifold
216: coordinates. Both local orbit coordinates change sign, so their
217: transition derivative stays positive. The same relative
218: line-coordinate construction therefore supplies the product over
219: the unchanged nodal elliptic factor, with boundary marking
220: \((H,w,K)\).
221: The exact real handle of P3K.6 gives a compression \(W\) from a
222: regular \((H,w)\)-torus at \(q=\epsilon\) to the fixed sphere,
223: whose projection lies over the real segment \([0,\epsilon]\).
224: Choose the regular binding at \(q=\epsilon\), outside the region
225: where the cusp page was modified above. The K-rotation of this
226: compression gives the same actual trace as the angular clutch
227: \(\theta K+3\theta(\theta-1)\delta\) of P3F.27.
228: 
229: The incoming map \(E'_\epsilon\times S^1_K\to T^3\) is the
230: actual diffeomorphism with the unimodular boundary marking
231: \(u=2H+K\), \(\gamma=-3H-K\). On the page boundary its inverse
232: angle is \(b-g_\epsilon(p)\), where \(g_\epsilon\) is the
233: real-valued incoming graph of the compression. Multiplying the
234: corresponding circle-valued function by a radial cutoff gives a
235: map from \(V\) to the inner parameter disc. The graph of that
236: map is embedded, and attaches to the rotating compression exactly
237: as in P3K.6. This gives the actual trace four-manifold \(X\).
238: 
239: The page need not miss the entire projected compression. The
240: following argument controls every remaining intersection. At
241: \(q=0\) the compression is the fixed sphere, and the constructed
242: page is disjoint from it. Compactness therefore gives \(q_a>0\)
243: such that their intersection has no point over \([0,q_a]\).
244: All remaining base values lie in \([q_a,\epsilon]\) and are
245: regular values of the source elliptic fibration. The three lifts
246: of this interval to the base page are disjoint regular bands
247: ending on its boundary.
248: 
249: Here is the boundary check for a normal pushoff, which is needed
250: because the incoming torus meets the whole binding. Near the
251: unchanged binding write the page collar as
252: \[
253:  q-\epsilon=\rho e^{i\alpha(\gamma)},\qquad \rho\geq0,
254: \]
255: where \(\alpha\) winds three times. Choose its normal pushoff
256: inside these unchanged base-coordinate annular and binding charts,
257: with base coordinate
258: \[
259:  q-\epsilon=(\rho+i\eta)e^{i\alpha(\gamma)},
260:  \qquad\eta>0.
261: \]
262: Intersection with the real compression requires
263: \(\rho=-\eta\cot\alpha\geq0\) and
264: \(q-\epsilon=-\eta/\sin\alpha\leq0\). Thus it is confined
265: to the three closed boundary arcs where
266: \(\pi/2\leq\alpha\leq\pi\). They are proper arcs of the
267: boundary circle. Enlarge the three lifted regular bands to contain
268: slightly larger arcs than the actual finite-collar intersections;
269: \(\alpha=\pi\) is only a limiting endpoint of the displayed
270: formula, and the three enlarged arcs still have disjoint gaps.
271: Each enlarged band is a half-disc attached to the
272: boundary, and it still contains no critical source value. The
273: pushoff can be taken small enough that the remainder of the page
274: misses the entire projected compression. More explicitly, on the
275: complement of the three bands the original page and the compact
276: projected compression are disjoint; retain a positive separation
277: there and make the pushoff smaller than this separation. Near the
278: binding the displayed formula gives the required separation
279: outside the three proper arcs. The radial homotopy keeps this
280: pushoff's \(Y\)-coordinate fixed, so its only possible
281: outer-annulus intersection is with the unrotated compression at
282: parameter angle zero. All such points lie in the three bands.
283: This gives actual neighbourhoods
284: \[
285:  U_i\cong T^2\times D_i,
286:  \qquad U_i\cap\overline{V\setminus U_i}
287:        \cong T^2\times A_i,
288: \]
289: where \(D_i\) is a half-disc and \(A_i\) its inner semicircle.
290: 
291: Let \(x\) be the unique meridian-one class on the complement of
292: the embedded trace in \(Y\times D^2\), as in P3K.6. Pull it back
293: by the framed graph-normal section. Outside the displayed bands
294: the exact radial homotopy of P3K.6 reaches the original parameter-zero
295: page pushoff without meeting the trace. Hence this pullback equals
296: the actual page-line Chern class there. But
297: \[
298:  H^2(T^2\times D_i,T^2\times A_i;\mathbb Z)=0,
299: \]
300: because \(A_i\hookrightarrow D_i\) is a homotopy equivalence.
301: Excision and the relative long exact sequence therefore make
302: restriction to the complement of all these bands injective in
303: degree two. The two line classes agree on the whole page. Possible
304: differences of relative lifts are boundary gauges; their squares
305: and their products with the page line vanish because its boundary
306: Chern class is zero. This proves the actual graph-normal line
307: comparison, including the boundary restriction.
308: 
309: ## 4. Characteristic calculation and the scope of the result
310: 
311: The compact elliptic page has \(\chi=1\), signature \(-8\),
312: and \(G\cdot O=0\). Consequently its section line has relative
313: square \(-2\), and the relative Pontryagin pairing is \(-24\).
314: Cap the trace as in P3K.7. The different incoming torus marking is
315: retained: the compression cap kills the primitive vanishing cycle
316: \(w\), while the regular elliptic cap kills the old gamma cycle.
317: The exact change of the remaining two cycles is
318: \[
319:  \begin{pmatrix}u&\gamma\end{pmatrix}
320:  =\begin{pmatrix}H&K\end{pmatrix}
321:    \begin{pmatrix}2&-3\\1&-1\end{pmatrix},
322:  \qquad\det\begin{pmatrix}2&-3\\1&-1\end{pmatrix}=1.
323: \]
324: It extends as a diffeomorphism of the two-torus factor of the
325: compression cap. With the original marked boundary the two caps
326: are exactly
327: \[
328:  H_w=D^2_w\times S^1_u\times S^1_\gamma,\qquad
329:  H_\gamma=S^1_w\times S^1_u\times D^2_\gamma.
330: \]
331: Their closed difference is \(S^1_u\times S^3\), by the two
332: solid-torus decomposition in the \((w,\gamma)\) coordinates.
333: In particular both caps have signature zero. The restriction
334: \(H^2(H_w;\mathbb Z)\to H^2(T^3;\mathbb Z)\) is injective,
335: so the zero boundary Chern class forces the cap line class to be
336: zero. A change of its gluing to the page is the image of a boundary
337: gauge; its square and product with the page line vanish by the
338: relative cup-product formula of P3K.5. Novikov additivity and the
339: signature theorem compute the Pontryagin pairing while retaining
340: both cap markings. The exact graph-normal comparison above
341: therefore gives
342: \[
343:  b^2=4(-2)=-8,\qquad p_1(T\bar X)=-24.
344: \]
345: The sphere-bundle calculation of P3K.7, with both return-orientation
346: reversals retained, gives
347: \[
348:  A=\frac{3b^2-p_1(T\bar X)}{24}=0.
349: \]
350: The positive \(r\)-loop has the actual K-clutch of P3F.27 for
351: this fan as well: its integer fan translations preserve
352: (P3F.31.1), and its bounded origin extends in these charts.
353: P3F.22 consequently gives zero class in \(\Theta_7\), and
354: P3F.24 gives the product mapping torus.
355: 
356: This calculation constructs the explicit positive-diagonal family.
357: P3K.17 subsequently proves that the negative-diagonal family in
358: P3F.26 has the same actual return, by its charged Cox bordism
359: including the extra fixed-locus sphere-bundle boundary. The supplied E26
360: Mumford proposition permits arbitrary periodic unimodular
361: triangulations and does not select one of these diagonals. Its
362: literal Remark8.5 requires a whole-fibre divisor, so bare OS49
363: with component divisor \(C_0\) is the additional configuration
364: separately requested by the prompt, rather than an instance of that
365: particular hypothesis.
